    {
      "node_id": "kg_doc_4",
      "node_type": "kg",
      "content": "The first known landing in Australia by Europeans was by Dutch navigator Willem Janszoon in 1606. Twenty - nine other Dutch navigators explored the western and southern coasts in the 17th century, and dubbed the continent New Holland. Macassan trepangers visited Australia's northern coasts after 1720, possibly earlier. Other European explorers followed until, in 1770, Lieutenant James Cook charted the east coast of Australia for Great Britain and returned with accounts favouring colonisation at Botany Bay (now in Sydney), New South Wales.",
      "depth": 0,
      "parent_id": null,
      "value": 0.0,
      "temperature": 0.0,
      "expanded": false,
      "visit_count": 0,
      "persistent": true,
